# mapbox/earcut.hpp — Z-order / hashing machinery

Everything below was **reconstructed from knowledge and then verified instruction-by-instruction against `/workspace/dataset/test19_executable`** (it is unstripped: `nm -C` exposes `mapbox::detail::Earcut<unsigned int>::{zOrder,indexCurve,isEarHashed,earcutLinked,...}`, and the `__assert_fail` string proves the source is `../earcut.hpp` with `assert(start)` at **line 593** inside `indexCurve`). Where the binary settles a question I mark it **[VERIFIED]** with the evidence.

---

## 0. Object layout facts needed by the rest (verified from disassembly)

`Earcut<N>` member offsets (from `operator()` @ `0x4047d0` and `zOrder` @ `0x4021f0`):

| offset | member |
|---|---|
| 0x00 | `std::vector<N> indices` |
| 0x18 | `std::size_t vertices` |
| 0x20 | `bool hashing` |
| 0x28 | `double minX` |
| 0x30 | `double maxX` |
| 0x38 | `double minY` |
| 0x40 | `double maxY` |
| 0x48 | `double inv_size` |
| 0x50 | node pool |

```cpp
    bool hashing;
    double minX, maxX;
    double minY, maxY;
    double inv_size = 0;
```

`Node` layout **[VERIFIED]** — stride is 56 bytes (`lea (,%rcx,8); sub %rcx; shl $3` = `56*i` in `splitPolygon` @ `0x403751`), and `filterPoints` reads steiner as `cmpb $0x0,0x27(%rdi); jns` i.e. **bit 31 of the dword at 0x24**:

```cpp
    struct Node {
        Node(N index, double x_, double y_) : x(x_), y(y_), i(index) {}
        Node(const Node&) = delete;
        Node& operator=(const Node&) = delete;
        Node(Node&&) = delete;
        Node& operator=(Node&&) = delete;

        const double x;          // 0x00
        const double y;          // 0x08
        Node* prev = nullptr;    // 0x10
        Node* next = nullptr;    // 0x18
        int32_t z = 0;           // 0x20   <-- z-order value, SIGNED 32-bit
        const N i : 31;          // 0x24 bits 0..30  (insertNode stores (i<<32)&0x7fffffff00000000)
        bool steiner : 1;        // 0x24 bit 31
        Node* prevZ = nullptr;   // 0x28
        Node* nextZ = nullptr;   // 0x30
    };                           // sizeof == 56
```

> Note: the classic upstream v2.2.4 listing declares `const N i;` **first** and `bool steiner = false;` last (sizeof 72). The binary here is unambiguously the compacted/bitfield ordering above. This is semantically irrelevant for a port *except* that `i` is masked to 31 bits (`and $0x7fffffff,%eax` before every `indices.emplace_back(...->i)`, e.g. `0x40461e`).

---

## 1. `indexCurve` — **exact**, verified

Disassembly `0x402280`:
- `mov 0x20(%rbx),%eax; test %eax,%eax; jne 4022b3` … `call zOrder` … **`4022b3: mov %eax,0x20(%rbx)`** — the store to `z` is on the **merge point of both paths**, i.e. an *unconditional assignment* whose RHS is a ternary. This confirms **`p->z = p->z ? p->z : zOrder(p->x, p->y);`**, *not* `if (p->z == 0) p->z = ...`.
- `movdqu 0x10(%rbx),%xmm2` (`prev`,`next`) → `movups %xmm2,0x28(%rbx)` (`prevZ`,`nextZ`) — the two links are copied as one 16-byte move.
- `mov 0x28(%r10),%rax; movq $0x0,0x30(%rax); movq $0x0,0x28(%r10)` — `p->prevZ->nextZ = nullptr; p->prevZ = nullptr;`
- `sortLinked` is **inlined** and its return value **discarded**.

```cpp
// interlink polygon nodes in z-order
template <typename N>
void Earcut<N>::indexCurve(Node* start) {
    assert(start);                      // earcut.hpp:593  [VERIFIED via __assert_fail]
    Node* p = start;

    do {
        p->z = p->z ? p->z : zOrder(p->x, p->y);
        p->prevZ = p->prev;
        p->nextZ = p->next;
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != start);

    p->prevZ->nextZ = nullptr;
    p->prevZ = nullptr;

    sortLinked(p);                      // return value intentionally discarded
}
```

Why the `z ? z : zOrder()` idiom matters for *this* self-intersecting input: `splitEarcut` calls `earcutLinked(a)` / `earcutLinked(c)` with **`pass = 0`** (`xor %edx,%edx` at `0x404371` / `0x404389`), so `indexCurve` runs **again** on each sub-ring. Nodes that already have a `z` keep it (byte-identical), while the two nodes freshly allocated by `splitPolygon` (which have `z == 0`, `prevZ == nextZ == nullptr` — `movl $0x0,0x20(%rbx)` at `0x403729`/`0x403785`) get a `z` computed then. A point whose true z-order happens to be 0 (i.e. exactly at `(minX,minY)`) is recomputed every time — harmless, same value.

---

## 2. `sortLinked` — full iterative bottom-up merge sort, **exact**, verified

Verified against the inlined body at `0x4022e2 … 0x4023a4`:
- `mov $0x1,%r9d` → `inSize = 1`; `add %r9d,%r9d` at the bottom → `inSize *= 2`.
- `r11d` = `numMerges` (`add $0x1,%r11d`), `eax` = `pSize`, `edi` = `qSize`.
- Tie-break at `0x402370`: `mov 0x20(%rdx),%ebx` (`q->z`), `cmp %ebx,0x20(%rsi)` (`p->z`), **`jg`** → take `q` only when `p->z > q->z`; i.e. source is **`p->z <= q->z`** and the comparison is **signed** (int32_t).
- Tail linking at `0x402333`: `test %r8,%r8; je → list = e; else tail->nextZ = e;` then `e->prevZ = tail; tail = e;`.
- `0x402390: movq $0x0,0x30(%r8)` — `tail->nextZ = nullptr` executed **unconditionally, unguarded** (GCC even emitted a `ud2` trap for the null-tail path at `0x4023aa`).
- `cmp $0x1,%r11d; je <return>` → `if (numMerges <= 1) return list;`

```cpp
// Simon Tatham's linked list merge sort algorithm
// http://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~sgtatham/algorithms/listsort.html
template <typename N>
typename Earcut<N>::Node*
Earcut<N>::sortLinked(Node* list) {
    assert(list);
    Node* p;
    Node* q;
    Node* e;
    Node* tail;
    int i, numMerges, pSize, qSize;
    int inSize = 1;

    for (;;) {
        p = list;
        list = nullptr;
        tail = nullptr;
        numMerges = 0;

        while (p) {
            numMerges++;
            q = p;
            pSize = 0;
            for (i = 0; i < inSize; i++) {
                pSize++;
                q = q->nextZ;
                if (!q) break;
            }

            qSize = inSize;

            while (pSize > 0 || (qSize > 0 && q)) {

                if (pSize == 0) {
                    e = q;
                    q = q->nextZ;
                    qSize--;
                } else if (qSize == 0 || !q) {
                    e = p;
                    p = p->nextZ;
                    pSize--;
                } else if (p->z <= q->z) {          // <= : STABLE, ties keep list order
                    e = p;
                    p = p->nextZ;
                    pSize--;
                } else {
                    e = q;
                    q = q->nextZ;
                    qSize--;
                }

                if (tail) tail->nextZ = e;
                else list = e;

                e->prevZ = tail;
                tail = e;
            }

            p = q;
        }

        tail->nextZ = nullptr;                       // no null-check on tail

        if (numMerges <= 1) return list;

        inSize *= 2;
    }
}
```

**Porting warning:** the `p->z <= q->z` tie-break makes the sort *stable*, and stability is load-bearing for byte-identical output on degenerate/duplicate-`z` inputs (which this fractal ring has, since 2n+2 points get quantized to a 32767×32767 grid). A `sort_by_key` that is not stable, or one using `<` instead of `<=`, will diverge. Also note `inSize` / `pSize` / `qSize` / `numMerges` are plain `int`.

Some older forks write the outer loop as `while (numMerges > 1)` with a `do{}while` shape; the observable behaviour is identical because the exit test is evaluated after `tail->nextZ = nullptr`. The `for(;;)` + `if (numMerges <= 1) return list;` form above is what this binary compiles to.

---

## 3. `zOrder` — **exact**, verified

Full disassembly of `_ZN6mapbox6detail6EarcutIjE6zOrderEdd` @ `0x4021f0`:

```
subsd  0x28(%rdi),%xmm0     ; x_ - minX
movsd  0x48(%rdi),%xmm2     ; inv_size
subsd  0x38(%rdi),%xmm1     ; y_ - minY
mulsd  %xmm2,%xmm0          ; * inv_size      <-- MULTIPLY, not divide
mulsd  %xmm2,%xmm1
cvttsd2si %xmm0,%edx        ; (int32_t) truncate toward zero, 32-bit dest
cvttsd2si %xmm1,%esi
... shl 8 / or / and 0xff00ff ; shl 4 / or / and 0xf0f0f0f
... lea(,r,4) / or / and 0x33333333 ; lea(r,r,1) / or / and 0x55555555
add %eax,%eax ; and $0xaaaaaaaa ; or %ecx,%eax    ; == x | (y << 1)
```

So the **modern C++ header multiplies by a precomputed `inv_size = 32767.0 / size`**. The literal `32767.0` is in `.rodata` at `0x4050a8` (`0x40DFFFC000000000`) and is used with **`divsd`** exactly once, in `operator()`.

```cpp
// z-order of a point given coords and inverse of the longer side of data bbox
template <typename N>
int32_t Earcut<N>::zOrder(const double x_, const double y_) {
    // coords are transformed into non-negative 15-bit integer range
    int32_t x = static_cast<int32_t>((x_ - minX) * inv_size);
    int32_t y = static_cast<int32_t>((y_ - minY) * inv_size);

    x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
    x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
    x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
    x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;

    y = (y | (y << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
    y = (y | (y << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
    y = (y | (y << 2)) & 0x33333333;
    y = (y | (y << 1)) & 0x55555555;

    return x | (y << 1);
}
```

Types, precisely:
- both locals are **`int32_t`** (signed) — proven by `cvttsd2si %xmm0,%edx` writing a **32-bit** register. A `uint32_t` cast would have produced `cvttsd2si …,%rax` (64-bit) on x86-64 GCC.
- Return type is **`int32_t`**; all comparisons on `z` downstream are **signed** (`jle`/`jg`).
- Masks are applied in the order **8 → 4 → 2 → 1** with masks **`0x00FF00FF` → `0x0F0F0F0F` → `0x33333333` → `0x55555555`**. (GCC folded the final `(y & 0x55555555) << 1` into `(y+y) & 0xAAAAAAAA`; same value.)
- Truncation is **toward zero** (`cvttsd2si`). Since `x_ >= minX` always, values land in `[0, 32767]`, so no negative/overflow case arises and `y << 1` never reaches bit 31. Max possible return is `0x3FFFFFFF`.

**Variants (for the historical record — this binary uses A):**

- **VARIANT A (modern C++ header, v2.2.x — CONFIRMED HERE):**
  `int32_t x = static_cast<int32_t>((x_ - minX) * inv_size);` with member `double inv_size` set to `32767. / size`.
- **VARIANT B (older earcut.hpp, member was `double size`):**
  `int32_t x = static_cast<int32_t>(32767.0 * (x_ - minX) / size);`
- **VARIANT C (earcut.js ≥ 2.2.3):** identical to A but written `x = (x - minX) * invSize | 0;` (JS `|0` = truncate toward zero, same as `cvttsd2si`).

A and B are *not* bit-identical in general: `32767*(d)/s` and `d*(32767/s)` round differently, so a boundary point can land on a different integer cell and therefore a different `z`. Use A.

---

## 4. `removeNode` — z-list maintenance, and where `z` is (not) reset

Verified from the inlined copy inside `earcutLinked`'s ear-cutting block (`0x404686`–`0x4046b9`) and inside `filterPoints` (`0x401986`–`0x4019ac`):

```
mov 0x10(%r12),%rax   ; p->prev
mov 0x18(%r12),%rdx   ; p->next
mov %rax,0x10(%rdx)   ; p->next->prev = p->prev
mov %rdx,0x18(%rax)   ; p->prev->next = p->next
mov 0x28(%r12),%rax   ; p->prevZ
test %rax,%rax ; je
  mov 0x30(%r12),%rdx ; p->nextZ
  mov %rdx,0x30(%rax) ; p->prevZ->nextZ = p->nextZ
mov 0x30(%r12),%rdx   ; p->nextZ
test %rdx,%rdx ; je
  mov %rax,0x28(%rdx) ; p->nextZ->prevZ = p->prevZ
```

```cpp
template <typename N>
void Earcut<N>::removeNode(Node* p) {
    p->next->prev = p->prev;
    p->prev->next = p->next;

    if (p->prevZ) p->prevZ->nextZ = p->nextZ;
    if (p->nextZ) p->nextZ->prevZ = p->prevZ;
}
```

**Is `z` ever reset? No.**
- `removeNode` unlinks the node from the z-chain but leaves the removed node's own `z`, `prevZ`, `nextZ` **stale/dangling**. That is safe only because a removed node is never revisited.
- The **only** writes to `z` in the whole binary are: (a) `indexCurve` (`0x4022bc`), and (b) node construction — `insertNode` (`0x4032d3`, `z=0` packed with `i` in one 8-byte store) and `splitPolygon`'s two new nodes (`0x403729`, `0x403785`).
- Therefore, once assigned in the first `indexCurve`, a surviving node's `z` is **frozen forever**, even across `splitEarcut` re-indexing. Any port that recomputes `z` after a split (e.g. because the sub-ring's bbox/`minX`/`inv_size` "should" be recomputed) will diverge — `minX/minY/inv_size` are computed **once** in `operator()` and never updated.
- `cureLocalIntersections` creates no nodes; it just calls `removeNode(p)` and `removeNode(p->next)`, so it only shortens the z-chain.
- New nodes from `splitPolygon` have `prevZ == nextZ == nullptr` until the *next* `indexCurve` re-links the whole sub-ring.

---

## 5. Guard on the hashed path — it is **`hashing`**, not `inv_size != 0`

**[VERIFIED]** `earcutLinked` @ `0x404460`:
```
404460: test %rsi,%rsi ; je ret            ; if (!ear) return;
40447f: test %edx,%edx ; jne skip          ; if (!pass
404483: cmpb $0x0,0x20(%rdi) ; jne ->      ;     && hashing) indexCurve(ear);   [0x20 == this->hashing]
...
4044e7: cmpb $0x0,0x20(%r14) ; jne isEarHashed   ; hashing ? isEarHashed(ear) : isEar(ear)
```

So:

```cpp
    if (!pass && hashing) indexCurve(ear);
    ...
        if (hashing ? isEarHashed(ear) : isEar(ear)) { ... }
```

`inv_size != 0` appears **only once**, inside `operator()`, purely to avoid a division by zero for a degenerate (zero-area-bbox) polygon:

```cpp
    // if the shape is not too simple, we'll use z-order curve hash later; calculate polygon bbox
    hashing = threshold < 0;
    if (hashing) {
        Node* p = outerNode->next;
        minX = maxX = outerNode->x;
        minY = maxY = outerNode->y;
        do {
            x = p->x;
            y = p->y;
            minX = std::min<double>(minX, x);
            minY = std::min<double>(minY, y);
            maxX = std::max<double>(maxX, x);
            maxY = std::max<double>(maxY, y);
            p = p->next;
        } while (p != outerNode);

        // minX, minY and inv_size are later used to transform coords into integers for z-order calculation
        inv_size = std::max<double>(maxX - minX, maxY - minY);
        inv_size = inv_size != .0 ? (32767. / inv_size) : .0;
    }
```

Disassembly evidence (`0x40492a`–`0x404962`, `0x404c10`): `maxsd` of the two extents → `ucomisd` against 0 with `jp/jne → 0x404c10`, where `movsd 32767.0; divsd %xmm2; movsd → 0x48(%rbp)`; the fall-through stores `+0.0`. Note the guard is `!= .0`, which is **false for NaN as well as ±0** — a NaN extent yields `inv_size = 0.0`.

Consequence: **if the bbox is degenerate, `hashing` stays `true`** and `isEarHashed` is still used, with every `z == 0`. This is different from earcut.js, where the single `invSize` variable serves as both the value and the flag, so `invSize == 0` falls back to `isEar`. A port must reproduce the C++ behaviour (separate `hashing` bool).

### The `hashing` threshold (relevant to the n≥40 crossover in this task)

**[VERIFIED]** `0x404819: mov $0x50,%ebx` (= 80), loop `sub %eax,%ebx; jns`, then `mov %ebx,%eax; shr $0x1f,%eax; mov %al,0x20(%rbp)`:

```cpp
    double x, y;
    int threshold = 80;
    std::size_t len = 0;

    for (size_t i = 0; threshold >= 0 && i < points.size(); i++) {
        threshold -= static_cast<int>(points[i].size());
        len += points[i].size();
    }

    // estimate size of nodes and indices
    nodes.reset(len * 3 / 2);                     // [VERIFIED: lea (r,r,2); shr]
    indices.reserve(len + points[0].size());      // [VERIFIED]

    Node* outerNode = linkedList(points[0], true);
    if (!outerNode || outerNode->prev == outerNode->next) return;

    if (points.size() > 1) outerNode = eliminateHoles(points, outerNode);

    hashing = threshold < 0;    // computed AFTER eliminateHoles
    ...
    earcutLinked(outerNode);
```

For a single ring of `2n+2` points: `hashing == (2n+2 > 80)`, i.e. **`n >= 40`**. Exactly matches the task description.

---

## Bonus — `isEarHashed` (this build is the TWO-loop variant, not the JS 2.2 bidirectional one)

This is important and easy to get wrong: earcut.js ≥ 2.2.0 walks `prevZ`/`nextZ` **interleaved** in one combined loop plus two cleanup loops (3 loop bodies). **This C++ binary has exactly two loops** — `nextZ` ascending first (`0x402683: cmp %eax,0x20(%rdx); jle`, walking `0x30`), then `prevZ` descending (`0x40268c: mov 0x28(%r13),%rax`). GCC does not synthesize that shape from the bidirectional source, so the header is the older, sequential form. Traversal order changes which candidate point rejects an ear first — irrelevant for the boolean result on well-formed input, but it **does** change nothing observable here since the predicate is a pure conjunction; still, port the two-loop form to stay faithful.

```cpp
template <typename N>
bool Earcut<N>::isEarHashed(Node* ear) {
    const Node* a = ear->prev;
    const Node* b = ear;
    const Node* c = ear->next;

    if (area(a, b, c) >= 0) return false; // reflex, can't be an ear

    // triangle bbox; min & max are calculated like this for speed
    const double minTX = std::min<double>(a->x, std::min<double>(b->x, c->x));
    const double minTY = std::min<double>(a->y, std::min<double>(b->y, c->y));
    const double maxTX = std::max<double>(a->x, std::max<double>(b->x, c->x));
    const double maxTY = std::max<double>(a->y, std::max<double>(b->y, c->y));

    // z-order range for the current triangle bbox;
    const int32_t minZ = zOrder(minTX, minTY);   // computed FIRST
    const int32_t maxZ = zOrder(maxTX, maxTY);   // computed SECOND

    // first look for points inside the triangle in increasing z-order
    Node* p = ear->nextZ;

    while (p && p->z <= maxZ) {
        if (p != ear->prev && p != ear->next &&
            pointInTriangle(a->x, a->y, b->x, b->y, c->x, c->y, p->x, p->y) &&
            area(p->prev, p, p->next) >= 0) return false;
        p = p->nextZ;
    }

    // then look for points in decreasing z-order
    p = ear->prevZ;

    while (p && p->z >= minZ) {
        if (p != ear->prev && p != ear->next &&
            pointInTriangle(a->x, a->y, b->x, b->y, c->x, c->y, p->x, p->y) &&
            area(p->prev, p, p->next) >= 0) return false;
        p = p->prevZ;
    }

    return true;
}
```

Verified details inside it:
- **no** triangle-bbox `p->x >= minTX && …` pre-filter (older 1.x had one; this build does not — the loop body jumps straight from the two pointer comparisons into `pointInTriangle`).
- the identity tests are `p != ear->prev && p != ear->next` (`cmp %rdx,%rbx` / `cmp %rdx,%rbp` at `0x4025a0`), **not** `p != a && p != c` — same nodes, but note they are re-read as `ear->prev`/`ear->next` in source.
- min/max are `std::min<double>(a->x, std::min<double>(b->x, c->x))` (nesting order confirmed at `0x40246d`–`0x402495`).
